olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Investors are reassessing the valu=
e of companies driving the artificial intelligence revolution</strong> afte=
r several leading AI figures called for a slowdown in development for safet=
y reasons.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Shares in AI-linked companies in Asia drop=
ped when trading began today, dragging South Korea=E2=80=99s KOSPI index do=
wn by 3.7%. Chipmaker SK Hynix has slumped by 5.75%</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">SoftBank, a big AI invester, fell by as mu=
ch as 13% in Tokyo after OpenAI=E2=80=99s chief executive officer, Sam Altm=
an, said that the ChatGPT-maker won=E2=80=99t go public this year (SoftBank=
 owns a stake in OpenAI). Taipei=E2=80=99s Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturi=
ng Company dropped by 1.2%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Traders=E2=80=99 optimism about AI has tak=
en a hit after the CEO of Anthropic, Dario Amodei, appealed for the AI indu=
stry to =E2=80=9Cslow down=E2=80=9D =E2=80=93 a call which was quickly back=
ed by both Altman and Elon Musk.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Amodei said =E2=80=9Cbuilding [AI] too fas=
t is reckless=E2=80=9D, and warned that a swarm of AI agents could cause hu=
ndreds of billions of dollars of damage by =E2=80=9Ctaking over the entire =
internet=E2=80=9D in future.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Amodei=E2=80=99s pacing proposal has <a hr=
ef=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG1=
8IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEA13-uHzzNLsl3ZqJFCJlFeoXoeFuI5KUGBoPxdYbPXIpOIfGzD4=
oMgYEbSsOLR7mSmdpKavce7NoiOdGd0iZHVnWA0CTL_VhYLozzEJp8ZuDVgP58AHVcE5cgMGL-O=
hJIgl_QtwelJNvAjr3nyRpPiAuRyOkn80FrECdTLn8pCgouaunLECnihw-JepvzyAkv0fqYA2tA=
CdNJEiOOX88xObwUKfLmduEZDYrOXVzoDeoaOapnncLrUUwkjMQ9u1GNpvzRkLIXGiZ2gtOcUDK=
buU6N2PGdO4rTuhQx_dHgP89BpUWF4HxzaF4skmwf2O_Y/4u1/ON52siQkSqCYKsCMD8AEmw/h3=
1/h001.QEE2DO6PC7ugTRU9Tbmi2Iw18Nh7Fn_vh91x-8tNFXA" style=3D"color: #0077B6=
;">drawn heavy scepticism from critics</a> and researchers who allege that =
AI firms are attempting to pre-empt more stringent government regulation an=
d retain the status quo of power within the industry.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Although Amodie=E2=80=99s claims have been=
 <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.SuMpm1IH9cIV=
BKyOtJ6VPXX4BVsp-H_huZMFBPAiu8psgQts_goWDHHBdaFXMX_ikl4uiNcjz_duAnU9SfGOf4u=
FgDxbgxP2o5wjZ5IjZqEZrhPtyuJafx6_AOverK9Ut5epV9oTQfMDE9R620K8JKt4LyARJG_t53=
nPo1RoiBWYt3OiMeRRPIMKKQf1PqjRP9vnl8gjO1mrZnWoY3eRnmLAHTifbUBqY_xrDyMutXxWd=
JbpclgFSS5RNkjqvCbn1F2bYdySS8M80XwK05aZyw/4u1/ON52siQkSqCYKsCMD8AEmw/h32/h0=
01.71eNg5I2eZb6YMhLnL1u0d25u-Mk9HvpoG4Rd3MrNZE" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">d=
isputed by some</a>, investors are pricing in a slowdown, which would make =
it harder for the industry to pay for its rapid rollout of datacentres.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Ipek Ozkardeskaya, senior analyst at Swiss=
quote, says there is a =E2=80=9Csour mood in the markets this morning=E2=80=
=9D.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CSo if the AI race slows materiall=
y, the key question becomes: who pays for all that infrastructure? The leas=
es, debt and power commitments remain even if expected compute demand and r=
evenue growth slow. And that could bring credit risk increasingly into the =
AI story, particularly for highly leveraged datacentre operators and lender=
s exposed to projects built on aggressive assumptions about future AI deman=
d, at a time when interest rates =E2=80=93 hence borrowing costs =E2=80=93 =
are expected to rise.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CWhat=E2=80=99s interesting is tha=
t the slowdown may come not because Big Tech is out of cash, or because inv=
estors won=E2=80=99t play along. It comes from the actual people who develo=
p these models.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">*********</p>
